  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "# 2.05 — Inversive Geometry and Möbius Maps\n\nThis notebook takes the complex route into non-Euclidean geometry. The setting is the **Riemann sphere**\n\n$$\\hat{\\mathbb C} = \\mathbb C \\cup \\{\\infty\\},$$\n\nthe complex plane with a single point at infinity adjoined — note *one* point, not a whole line as in $\\mathbb{RP}^2$. That difference matters and we will come back to it.\n\nThe group is the **Möbius group**\n\n$$z \\longmapsto \\frac{az+b}{cz+d}, \\qquad ad - bc \\ne 0,$$\n\nwhich is $PGL_2(\\mathbb C)$, and over $\\mathbb C$ can be normalised to $ad-bc=1$, giving $PSL_2(\\mathbb C)$.\n\nThree facts make this geometry worth its own notebook:\n\n**Circles and lines stop being different things.** A Möbius map sends circles to circles, *provided* we agree that a line is a circle through $\\infty$. On the sphere this is literally true: under stereographic projection, lines in the plane become circles through the north pole. The distinction between \"straight\" and \"curved\" was an artefact of choosing where to stand.\n\n**Angles are preserved.** Möbius maps are conformal. So although this group destroys distance — as any group containing $z \\mapsto 2z$ must — it keeps angle. That is a different trade from the projective one, and it is what makes this geometry useful for analysis and for maps of the Earth.\n\n**Inversion generates it.** Just as reflections generated the isometry group in 1.02, *inversions in circles* generate the Möbius group together with its orientation-reversing partners. The analogy with 1.02 is exact, and 1.02's three-reflection theorem has a counterpart here.\n\nThe cross-ratio returns as the central invariant, and by the end we will have the tools to define hyperbolic distance in 2.06.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## 1. Möbius maps and the four generators\n\nEvery Möbius map factors into elementary pieces. Provided $c \\ne 0$,\n\n$$\\frac{az+b}{cz+d} = \\frac{a}{c} + \\frac{bc-ad}{c^2}\\cdot\\frac{1}{z + d/c},$$\n\nwhich reads as: translate, invert, scale-and-rotate, translate. So the group is generated by\n\n| Generator | Effect |\n|---|---|\n| $z \\mapsto z + b$ | translation |\n| $z \\mapsto \\lambda z$ ($\\lambda \\in \\mathbb C^*$) | rotation and scaling |\n| $z \\mapsto 1/z$ | inversion composed with a reflection |\n\nThe first two are affine and unremarkable. Everything genuinely new comes from $z \\mapsto 1/z$.\n\n**Matrix form.** Composition of Möbius maps is matrix multiplication:\n\n$$z \\mapsto \\frac{az+b}{cz+d} \\quad\\longleftrightarrow\\quad \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{pmatrix},$$\n\nwith the matrix determined up to scale — hence $PGL_2(\\mathbb C)$. This is the same identification we used in 2.03 for projectivities of a line, now over $\\mathbb C$, and it means a Möbius map *is* a projectivity of $\\mathbb{CP}^1$. The Riemann sphere and the complex projective line are the same object:\n\n$$\\hat{\\mathbb C} \\;=\\; \\mathbb{CP}^1 .$$\n\nThat explains the \"one point at infinity\": $\\mathbb{CP}^1$ has one point outside the affine chart, exactly as $\\mathbb{RP}^1$ does. It is $\\mathbb{RP}^2$ that has a whole line at infinity, because it is 2-dimensional over $\\mathbb R$ in a different way.\n\n**Three points determine the map.** By 2.03's counting, $PGL_2$ has 3 complex parameters, so there is a unique Möbius map sending any three distinct points to any other three. The standard choice sends $(z_1, z_2, z_3) \\mapsto (0, 1, \\infty)$, and the map that does it is — unsurprisingly — the cross-ratio.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "load(\"numerics\")$\nload(\"ax-plots\")$\nload(\"linearalgebra\")$\nratprint : false$\n\n/* A Möbius map as a 2×2 matrix, acting on the extended complex plane */\nmob(M, z) := block([a,b,c,d],\n  a : M[1][1], b : M[1][2], c : M[2][1], d : M[2][2],\n  if z = inf then (if c = 0 then inf else ratsimp(a/c))\n  elseif ratsimp(c*z + d) = 0 then inf\n  else ratsimp( (a*z + b)/(c*z + d) ) )$\n\ncompose(M, N) := ratsimp(M . N)$\n\n/* Composition of maps is multiplication of matrices */\nM1 : matrix([1, 2], [0, 1])$        /* z ↦ z + 2      */\nM2 : matrix([0, 1], [1, 0])$        /* z ↦ 1/z        */\nM3 : matrix([3, 0], [0, 1])$        /* z ↦ 3z         */\n\nprint(\"z ↦ z+2 then ↦ 1/z, at z = 5:\")$\nprint(\"   step by step : \", mob(M2, mob(M1, 5)))$\nprint(\"   composed     : \", mob(compose(M2, M1), 5))$\n\n/* The point at infinity behaves */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"under z ↦ 1/z :   0 ↦ \", mob(M2, 0), \"     ∞ ↦ \", mob(M2, inf))$\nprint(\"under z ↦ z+2 :   ∞ ↦ \", mob(M1, inf))$\nprint(\"under z ↦ (2z+1)/(z−3):  3 ↦ \", mob(matrix([2,1],[1,-3]), 3),\n      \"     ∞ ↦ \", mob(matrix([2,1],[1,-3]), inf))$\n\n/* The cross-ratio, as the unique map sending z1,z2,z3 → 0,1,∞ */\ncrmap(z1, z2, z3) := matrix([ z2 - z3, -z1*(z2 - z3) ],\n                            [ z2 - z1, -z3*(z2 - z1) ])$\nCM : crmap(4, 7, -2)$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"the map sending (4, 7, −2) to (0, 1, ∞):\")$\nprint(\"   4 ↦ \", mob(CM, 4), \"     7 ↦ \", mob(CM, 7), \"     −2 ↦ \", mob(CM, -2))$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 9,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "z ↦ z+2 then ↦ 1/z, at z = 5:\n   step by step :  1/7\n   composed     :  1/7\nunder z ↦ 1/z :   0 ↦  inf      ∞ ↦  0\nunder z ↦ z+2 :   ∞ ↦  inf\nunder z ↦ (2z+1)/(z−3):  3 ↦  inf      ∞ ↦  2\nthe map sending (4, 7, −2) to (0, 1, ∞):\n   4 ↦  0      7 ↦  1      −2 ↦  inf"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## 2. Circles go to circles\n\nWrite the general circle-or-line in the plane as\n\n$$\\alpha(x^2 + y^2) + Bx + Cy + \\gamma = 0,$$\n\nwith $\\alpha, B, C, \\gamma$ real. When $\\alpha \\ne 0$ this is a circle; when $\\alpha = 0$ it degenerates to a line. Calling both \"circles\" is the move that makes the theory uniform, and the sphere justifies it.\n\nSince translations and scalings obviously preserve this family, only $z \\mapsto 1/z$ needs checking. In complex form,\n\n$$\\frac1z = \\frac{\\bar z}{|z|^2}, \\qquad\\text{so}\\qquad (x,y) \\longmapsto \\left(\\frac{x}{x^2+y^2},\\; \\frac{-y}{x^2+y^2}\\right).$$\n\nSubstituting and clearing denominators should return an equation of exactly the same shape — with $\\alpha$ and $\\gamma$ exchanged. That swap is the entire content of the theorem, and it says precisely:\n\n| Before | $\\alpha$ | $\\gamma$ | After |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| circle **not** through $0$ | $\\ne 0$ | $\\ne 0$ | circle not through $0$ |\n| circle **through** $0$ ($\\gamma = 0$) | $\\ne 0$ | $0$ | **line** not through $0$ |\n| line **not** through $0$ ($\\alpha = 0$) | $0$ | $\\ne 0$ | circle through $0$ |\n| line **through** $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | line through $0$ |\n\nSo \"circle\" and \"line\" are not preserved separately — only their union is. A line is a circle that happens to pass through $\\infty$, and inversion moves $\\infty$ to $0$, which is why circles through the origin are exactly the ones that straighten out.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* The general circle-or-line, and the inversion z ↦ 1/z in real coordinates */\ngen_circle(al, B, C, ga, x, y) := al*(x^2 + y^2) + B*x + C*y + ga$\n\n/* z ↦ 1/z sends (x,y) to (x, −y)/(x²+y²).  Substitute and clear denominators. */\nE  : gen_circle(al, B, C, ga, u/(u^2+v^2), -v/(u^2+v^2))$\nE2 : ratsimp( E * (u^2 + v^2) )$\nprint(\"after substituting and multiplying by (u²+v²):\")$\nprint(\"   \", expand(E2))$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"compare with the general form in (u,v):\")$\nprint(\"   \", expand(gen_circle(ga, B, -C, al, u, v)))$\nprint(\"difference: \", ratsimp( expand(E2) - expand(gen_circle(ga, B, -C, al, u, v)) ))$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"so (α, B, C, γ) ↦ (γ, B, −C, α):  the same family, with α and γ swapped.\")$\n\n/* The four cases of the table, concretely */\nshow(al, B, C, ga, label) := block([kind, nal, nga],\n  nal : ga, nga : al,\n  kind(a, g) := if a = 0 then (if g = 0 then \"line through 0\" else \"line not through 0\")\n                else (if g = 0 then \"circle through 0\" else \"circle not through 0\"),\n  print(\"   \", label, \": \", kind(al, ga), \"  ↦  \", kind(nal, nga)) )$\n\nprint(\"\")$\nshow(1, 0, 0, -4, \"x²+y² = 4      \")$\nshow(1, -2, 0, 0,  \"x²+y² = 2x     \")$\nshow(0, 1, 0, -3,  \"x = 3          \")$\nshow(0, 1, -1, 0,  \"y = x          \")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 10,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "after substituting and multiplying by (u²+v²):\n    ga*v^2-C*v+ga*u^2+B*u+al\ncompare with the general form in (u,v):\n    ga*v^2-C*v+ga*u^2+B*u+al\ndifference:  0\nso (α, B, C, γ) ↦ (γ, B, −C, α):  the same family, with α and γ swapped.\n    x²+y² = 4       :  circle not through 0   ↦   circle not through 0\n    x²+y² = 2x      :  circle through 0   ↦   line not through 0\n    x = 3           :  line not through 0   ↦   circle through 0\n    y = x           :  line through 0   ↦   line through 0"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "### The concyclicity test\n\nThe complex cross-ratio gives a criterion that would be awkward to state any other way:\n\n> **Four points of $\\hat{\\mathbb C}$ lie on a common circle or line if and only if their cross-ratio is real.**\n\nThe proof is a two-liner given what we have. Send three of the points to $0, 1, \\infty$ by a Möbius map — those three lie on the real line, together with $\\infty$. The fourth point goes to $\\lambda$, the cross-ratio. The image four points are concyclic exactly when $\\lambda$ also lies on $\\mathbb R \\cup \\{\\infty\\}$, i.e. when $\\lambda$ is real. And since Möbius maps preserve both cross-ratio and the family of circles, the same holds before the map.\n\nRead the other way round, this *is* the circles-to-circles theorem, obtained from cross-ratio invariance instead of by substitution — a second proof, and a shorter one.\n\nIt also explains what the real line is doing in this picture. $\\mathbb R \\cup \\{\\infty\\}$ is just one circle among all the others; there is nothing distinguished about it until we choose to distinguish it. Choosing it — and keeping only the Möbius maps that preserve it *and* its upper side — is exactly how 2.06 will build the hyperbolic plane.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* Complex cross-ratio */\ncrC(z1,z2,z3,z4) := ratsimp( ((z3-z1)*(z4-z2)) / ((z3-z2)*(z4-z1)) )$\nis_real(w) := is( ratsimp(imagpart(rectform(w))) = 0 )$\n\n/* Four points on the unit circle */\nc4 : [1, %i, -1, -%i]$\nprint(\"four points on the unit circle: \", c4)$\nprint(\"   cross-ratio = \", crC(c4[1],c4[2],c4[3],c4[4]),\n      \"   real? \", is_real(crC(c4[1],c4[2],c4[3],c4[4])))$\n\n/* Four more, less symmetric, still on the unit circle (Pythagorean points) */\nc4b : [1, %i, (3+4*%i)/5, (-5+12*%i)/13]$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"four less symmetric points on the unit circle: \", c4b)$\nprint(\"   all on |z| = 1? \", map(lambda([z], is(ratsimp(abs(z)) = 1)), c4b))$\nlamb : crC(c4b[1],c4b[2],c4b[3],c4b[4])$\nprint(\"   cross-ratio = \", rectform(lamb), \"   real? \", is_real(lamb))$\n\n/* Four points on a LINE — also 'concyclic' in the extended sense */\nl4 : [0, 1, 3, 7]$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"four points on the real line: \", l4)$\nprint(\"   cross-ratio = \", crC(l4[1],l4[2],l4[3],l4[4]),\n      \"   real? \", is_real(crC(l4[1],l4[2],l4[3],l4[4])))$\n\n/* Four points NOT on any common circle */\nn4 : [0, 1, %i, 1+2*%i]$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"four points not concyclic: \", n4)$\nmu : crC(n4[1],n4[2],n4[3],n4[4])$\nprint(\"   cross-ratio = \", rectform(mu), \"   real? \", is_real(mu))$\n\n/* Invariance: apply a Möbius map and the cross-ratio is unchanged */\nMtest : matrix([1+%i, 2], [3, 1-%i])$\nimg : map(lambda([z], mob(Mtest, z)), c4b)$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"after a Möbius map, the concyclic four become: \", map(rectform, img))$\nprint(\"   their cross-ratio = \", rectform(crC(img[1],img[2],img[3],img[4])))$\nprint(\"   unchanged, hence still concyclic — circles go to circles\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 11,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "four points on the unit circle:  [1,%i,-1,-%i]\n   cross-ratio =  2    real?  true\nfour less symmetric points on the unit circle:\n  [1,%i,(4*%i+3)/5,(12*%i-5)/13]\n   all on |z| = 1?  [true,true,true,true]\n   cross-ratio =  -(1/3)    real?  true\nfour points on the real line:  [0,1,3,7]\n   cross-ratio =  9/7    real?  true\nfour points not concyclic:  [0,1,%i,2*%i+1]\n   cross-ratio =  3/5-%i/5    real?  false\nafter a Möbius map, the concyclic four become:\n  [(7*%i)/17+11/17,3/5-%i/5,%i/7+5/7,11/41-(17*%i)/41]\n   their cross-ratio =  -(1/3)\n   unchanged, hence still concyclic — circles go to circles"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## 3. Angles survive: conformality\n\nMöbius maps destroy distance but preserve angle. The reason is the one fact that makes complex analysis geometric:\n\n> A map that is complex-differentiable with $f'(z_0) \\ne 0$ acts, to first order, as multiplication by the complex number $f'(z_0)$ — and multiplication by a complex number is a **rotation composed with a scaling**.\n\nRotations and scalings both preserve angles, so $f$ does too. Concretely, if $f = u + iv$ then the real Jacobian is\n\n$$J = \\begin{pmatrix} u_x & u_y \\\\ v_x & v_y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} p & -q \\\\ q & p\\end{pmatrix}, \\qquad f' = p + iq,$$\n\nand that shape — the Cauchy–Riemann equations $u_x = v_y$, $u_y = -v_x$ — is exactly \"similarity matrix\". Compare 1.02, where $A^{\\mathsf T}A = I$ characterised the isometries; here $J$ is a scalar multiple of a rotation, so it is a *similarity*, preserving angle but not length.\n\nNote also that Möbius maps preserve **orientation** — $\\det J = p^2 + q^2 > 0$ — so they are the *conformal* half of the picture. The orientation-reversing counterparts involve $\\bar z$, and among them is genuine circle inversion, which is the subject of §4.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* Verify the Cauchy–Riemann equations for a Möbius map, symbolically.\n   Take real coefficients so the algebra stays readable; the complex case\n   is identical in form. */\ndeclare([aa,bb,cc,dd,x,y], real)$\nw  : (aa*(x + %i*y) + bb) / (cc*(x + %i*y) + dd)$\nuu : ratsimp( realpart( rectform(w) ) )$\nvv : ratsimp( imagpart( rectform(w) ) )$\n\nCR1 : ratsimp( diff(uu,x) - diff(vv,y) )$\nCR2 : ratsimp( diff(uu,y) + diff(vv,x) )$\nprint(\"Cauchy–Riemann residuals:\")$\nprint(\"   u_x − v_y = \", CR1)$\nprint(\"   u_y + v_x = \", CR2)$\n\n/* So the Jacobian is [[p, −q],[q, p]] — a scaling times a rotation */\nJ : matrix([diff(uu,x), diff(uu,y)], [diff(vv,x), diff(vv,y)])$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"det J = \", ratsimp(determinant(J)), \"   (positive: orientation preserved)\")$\nprint(\"J Jᵀ = (scale²)·I ?  off-diagonal entry = \",\n      ratsimp( (J . transpose(J))[1][2] ))$\n\n/* An angle, checked concretely: two curves through a point, before and after */\nf(z) := (z + 2)/(z - %i)$\nz0 : 1 + %i$\n/* tangent directions of two curves through z0 */\nt1 : 1$          /* horizontal */\nt2 : %i$         /* vertical — a right angle between them */\nfp : ratsimp( subst(z = z0, diff((z + 2)/(z - %i), z)) )$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"f'(z0) = \", rectform(fp))$\nprint(\"images of the two tangent directions: \",\n      rectform(fp*t1), \"  and  \", rectform(fp*t2))$\nang(u1, u2) := float( carg(rectform(u2/u1)) * 180/%pi )$\nprint(\"angle before = \", ang(t1, t2), \"°     angle after = \",\n      ang(fp*t1, fp*t2), \"°   — preserved\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 12,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "Cauchy–Riemann residuals:\n   u_x − v_y =  0\n   u_y + v_x =  0\ndet J =\n        (aa^2*dd^2-2*aa*bb*cc*dd+bb^2*cc^2)\n         /(cc^4*y^4+(2*cc^4*x^2+4*cc^3*dd*x+2*cc^2*dd^2)*y^2+cc^4*x^4\n                   +4*cc^3*dd*x^3+6*cc^2*dd^2*x^2+4*cc*dd^3*x+dd^4)\n           (positive: orientation preserved)\nJ Jᵀ = (scale²)·I ?  off-diagonal entry =  0\nf'(z0) =  -%i-2\nimages of the two tangent directions:  -%i-2   and   1-2*%i\nangle before =  90.0 °     angle after =  90.0 °   — preserved"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## 4. Inversion, and the analogy with reflection\n\n**Inversion** in the circle of centre $a$ and radius $r$ is\n\n$$\\iota(z) = a + \\frac{r^2}{\\overline{z - a}} .$$\n\nIts properties line up, one for one, with those of a reflection in 1.02:\n\n| Reflection in a line (1.02) | Inversion in a circle |\n|---|---|\n| fixes the line pointwise | fixes the circle pointwise |\n| an involution, $F^2 = I$ | an involution, $\\iota^2 = \\mathrm{id}$ |\n| swaps the two half-planes | swaps inside and outside |\n| orientation-reversing | orientation-reversing |\n| composing two gives a rotation or translation | composing two gives a Möbius map |\n| **generates $E(2)$** | **generates the Möbius group (with reflections)** |\n\nThe last row is the point. In 1.02, reflections generated everything and the classification followed from counting them. Here the same holds: the orientation-preserving maps are the products of an *even* number of inversions, and those are exactly the Möbius maps. A reflection in a line is itself the degenerate case — inversion in a \"circle\" of infinite radius.\n\nThe defining property $|\\iota(z) - a|\\cdot|z - a| = r^2$ says inversion trades distance-from-centre for its reciprocal, which is why it turns the inside of the circle into the outside and sends the centre to $\\infty$.\n\nInversion is where the geometry stops being about $\\mathbb C$ as a field and starts being about $\\hat{\\mathbb C}$ as a *sphere* — and that is the last thing to check.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* Inversion in the circle |z − a| = r */\ninv(a, r, z) := if z = inf then a\n                elseif ratsimp(z - a) = 0 then inf\n                else ratsimp( a + r^2 / conjugate(z - a) )$\n\n/* It fixes the circle pointwise */\nprint(\"inversion in |z| = 2:\")$\nfor z in [2, 2*%i, -2, (6+8*%i)/5] do\n  print(\"   \", z, \" ↦ \", ratsimp(inv(0, 2, z)), \"   fixed? \",\n        is( ratsimp(inv(0,2,z) - z) = 0 ))$\n\n/* It is an involution, and swaps inside with outside */\nprint(\"\")$\nfor z in [1, 4, 1+%i] do\n  print(\"   \", z, \" ↦ \", ratsimp(inv(0,2,z)),\n        \" ↦ \", ratsimp(inv(0,2,inv(0,2,z))),\n        \"     |z| = \", float(abs(z)), \" ↦ \", float(abs(inv(0,2,z))))$\n\n/* Centre and infinity are exchanged */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   0 ↦ \", inv(0,2,0), \"        ∞ ↦ \", inv(0,2,inf))$\nprint(\"   |ι(z) − a|·|z − a| = r² : \",\n      ratsimp( abs(inv(0,2,1+%i)) * abs(1+%i) ), \"  (should be 4)\")$\n\n/* Two inversions in concentric circles compose to a scaling — a Möbius map */\ncomp2(z) := ratsimp( inv(0, 3, inv(0, 2, z)) )$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"inversion in |z|=2, then in |z|=3:\")$\nfor z in [1+%i, 2, 5*%i] do print(\"   \", z, \" ↦ \", comp2(z))$\nprint(\"   — that is z ↦ (9/4)z, a scaling: orientation restored,\")$\nprint(\"     and the composition of two inversions is a Möbius map.\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 13,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "inversion in |z| = 2:\n    2  ↦  2    fixed?  true\n    2*%i  ↦  2*%i    fixed?  true\n    -2  ↦  -2    fixed?  true\n    (8*%i+6)/5  ↦  -(10/(4*%i-3))    fixed?  true\n    1  ↦  4  ↦  1      |z| =  1.0  ↦  4.0\n    4  ↦  1  ↦  4      |z| =  4.0  ↦  1.0\n    %i+1  ↦  -(4/(%i-1))  ↦  %i+1      |z| =  1.4142135623730951  ↦\n   2.8284271247461907\n   0 ↦  inf         ∞ ↦  0\n   |ι(z) − a|·|z − a| = r² :  4   (should be 4)\ninversion in |z|=2, then in |z|=3:\n    %i+1  ↦  (9*%i+9)/4\n    2  ↦  9/2\n    5*%i  ↦  (45*%i)/4\n   — that is z ↦ (9/4)z, a scaling: orientation restored,\n     and the composition of two inversions is a Möbius map."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## 5. The sphere behind it all\n\n**Stereographic projection** maps the unit sphere minus its north pole $N = (0,0,1)$ onto the plane, by drawing the line from $N$ through a point of the sphere and taking where it crosses $z = 0$:\n\n$$(X,Y,Z) \\longmapsto \\frac{X + iY}{1 - Z}, \\qquad\\text{with inverse}\\qquad z \\longmapsto \\left(\\frac{2x}{1+|z|^2},\\ \\frac{2y}{1+|z|^2},\\ \\frac{|z|^2-1}{|z|^2+1}\\right).$$\n\nSending $N \\mapsto \\infty$ makes it a bijection $S^2 \\to \\hat{\\mathbb C}$, and this is what the Riemann sphere *is*.\n\nA circle on the sphere is its intersection with a plane $aX + bY + cZ = d$. Substituting the inverse projection should turn that plane equation into the general circle-or-line equation of §2 — and the degenerate case $\\alpha = 0$ should occur exactly when the plane passes through $N$, i.e. when $c = d$.\n\nIf that works out, then:\n\n> **Lines in the plane are precisely the images of circles through the north pole.**\n\nWhich retires the distinction between lines and circles completely. They were the same objects all along; \"straight\" merely meant \"passing through the point we removed\".\n\nThis also gives the cleanest way to see why $\\hat{\\mathbb C}$ has one point at infinity rather than a line of them: the sphere has one north pole. And it identifies the Möbius group with the rotations of the sphere in the sub-case $PSU(2)$ — connecting back to 1.03's $SU(2) \\to SO(3)$ double cover, which is the same group appearing for the same reason.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* Inverse stereographic projection: plane (x,y) → sphere (X,Y,Z) */\ns   : x^2 + y^2$\nXs  : 2*x/(1 + s)$\nYs  : 2*y/(1 + s)$\nZs  : (s - 1)/(s + 1)$\n\n/* the image really is on the unit sphere */\nprint(\"X² + Y² + Z² = \", ratsimp(Xs^2 + Ys^2 + Zs^2))$\n\n/* A circle on the sphere = intersection with the plane aX + bY + cZ = d.\n   Substitute and clear denominators. */\nplane_eq : ratsimp( (aa*Xs + bb*Ys + cc*Zs - dd) * (1 + s) )$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"the plane equation pulled back to the (x,y) plane:\")$\nprint(\"   \", expand(plane_eq))$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"compare with the general circle (α)(x²+y²) + Bx + Cy + γ:\")$\nprint(\"   α = \", cc - dd, \"   B = \", 2*aa, \"   C = \", 2*bb, \"   γ = \", -(cc + dd))$\nprint(\"   difference = \",\n      ratsimp( expand(plane_eq)\n               - expand( (cc-dd)*(x^2+y^2) + 2*aa*x + 2*bb*y - (cc+dd) ) ))$\n\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"so the image is a LINE exactly when α = c − d = 0, i.e. c = d.\")$\nprint(\"the plane aX + bY + cZ = d passes through N = (0,0,1) iff c = d.\")$\nprint(\"⟹ lines in the plane are the images of circles through the north pole ∎\")$\n\n/* Concretely: the equator, a circle through N, and a circle avoiding N */\nshow_case(a0,b0,c0,d0,label) := print(\"   \", label,\n  \":  α = \", c0-d0, if c0-d0 = 0 then \"  → a LINE\" else \"  → a circle\",\n  \"   (through N? \", is(c0 = d0), \")\")$\nprint(\"\")$\nshow_case(0,0,1,0,   \"equator      Z = 0        \")$\nshow_case(0,0,1,1,   \"through N    Z = 1        \")$\nshow_case(1,0,1,1,   \"through N    X + Z = 1    \")$\nshow_case(0,0,1,1/2, \"avoiding N   Z = 1/2      \")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 14,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "X² + Y² + Z² =  1\nthe plane equation pulled back to the (x,y) plane:\n    -(dd*y^2)+cc*y^2+2*bb*y-dd*x^2+cc*x^2+2*aa*x-dd-cc\ncompare with the general circle (α)(x²+y²) + Bx + Cy + γ:\n   α =  cc-dd    B =  2*aa    C =  2*bb    γ =  -dd-cc\n   difference =  0\nso the image is a LINE exactly when α = c − d = 0, i.e. c = d.\nthe plane aX + bY + cZ = d passes through N = (0,0,1) iff c = d.\n⟹ lines in the plane are the images of circles through the north pole ∎\n    equator      Z = 0         :  α =  1   → a circle    (through N?  false )\n    through N    Z = 1         :  α =  0   → a LINE    (through N?  true )\n    through N    X + Z = 1     :  α =  0   → a LINE    (through N?  true )\n    avoiding N   Z = 1/2       :  α =  1/2   → a circle    (through N?  false\n   )"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* A grid of lines, and its image under z ↦ 1/z.\n   Every line missing the origin becomes a circle through the origin. */\ninv1(p) := block([d], d : p[1]^2 + p[2]^2,\n                  if d = 0 then [0.0, 0.0] else float([p[1]/d, -p[2]/d]))$\n\ntvals : makelist(k/6, k, -180, 180)$\nas_curve(ps) := [discrete, map(first, ps), map(second, ps)]$\n\nvline(k) := as_curve( map(lambda([t], float([k, t])), tvals) )$\nhline(k) := as_curve( map(lambda([t], float([t, k])), tvals) )$\nvimg(k)  := as_curve( map(lambda([t], inv1([k, t])), tvals) )$\nhimg(k)  := as_curve( map(lambda([t], inv1([t, k])), tvals) )$\n\nks : [1, 2, 3, -1, -2]$\nprint(\"line x = k  ↦  circle through 0, centre (1/2k, 0), radius 1/|2k|\")$\nfor k in ks do print(\"   k = \", k, \" → centre \", float([1/(2*k), 0]),\n                     \"  radius \", float(1/abs(2*k)))$\n\nplot2d( append( map(vline, ks), map(hline, ks),\n                map(vimg, ks),  map(himg, ks) ),\n        [x, -3.2, 3.2], [y, -3.2, 3.2],\n        [legend, false], [same_xy, true],\n        [title, \"z ↦ 1/z : a grid of lines becomes circles through the origin\"])$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 15,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "line x = k  ↦  circle through 0, centre (1/2k, 0), radius 1/|2k|\n   k =  1  → centre  [0.5,0.0]   radius  0.5\n   k =  2  → centre  [0.25,0.0]   radius  0.25\n   k =  3  → centre  [0.16666666666666666,0.0]   radius  0.16666666666666666\n   k =  -1  → centre  [-0.5,0.0]   radius  0.5\n   k =  -2  → centre  [-0.25,0.0]   radius  0.25"
          ]
        },
        {
          "data": {
            "image/svg+xml": [
              "<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"utf-8\"  standalone=\"no\"?>\n<svg \n width=\"600\" height=\"480\"\n viewBox=\"0 0 600 480\"\n xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"\n xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"\n>\n\n<title>Gnuplot</title>\n<desc>Produced by GNUPLOT 6.0 patchlevel 4 </desc>\n\n<g id=\"gnuplot_canvas\">\n\n<rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"600\" height=\"480\" fill=\"none\"/>\n<defs>\n\n\t<circle id='gpDot' r='0.5' stroke-width='0.5' stroke='currentColor'/>\n\t<path id='gpPt0' stroke-width='0.222' stroke='currentColor' d='M-1,0 h2 M0,-1 v2'/>\n\t<path id='gpPt1' stroke-width='0.222' stroke='currentColor' d='M-1,-1 L1,1 M1,-1 L-1,1'/>\n\t<path id='gpPt2' stroke-width='0.222' stroke='currentColor' d='M-1,0 L1,0 M0,-1 L0,1 M-1,-1 L1,1 M-1,1 L1,-1'/>\n\t<rect id='gpPt3' stroke-width='0.222' stroke='currentColor' x='-1' y='-1' width='2' height='2'/>\n\t<rect id='gpPt4' stroke-width='0.222' stroke='currentColor' fill='currentColor' x='-1' y='-1' width='2' height='2'/>\n\t<circle id='gpPt5' stroke-width='0.222' stroke='currentColor' cx='0' cy='0' r='1'/>\n\t<use xlink:href='#gpPt5' id='gpPt6' fill='currentColor' stroke='none'/>\n\t<path id='gpPt7' stroke-width='0.222' stroke='currentColor' d='M0,-1.33 L-1.33,0.67 L1.33,0.67 z'/>\n\t<use xlink:href='#gpPt7' id='gpPt8' fill='currentColor' stroke='none'/>\n\t<use xlink:href='#gpPt7' id='gpPt9' stroke='currentColor' transform='rotate(180)'/>\n\t<use xlink:href='#gpPt9' id='gpPt10' fill='currentColor' stroke='none'/>\n\t<use xlink:href='#gpPt3' id='gpPt11' stroke='currentColor' transform='rotate(45)'/>\n\t<use xlink:href='#gpPt11' id='gpPt12' fill='currentColor' stroke='none'/>\n\t<path id='gpPt13' stroke-width='0.222' stroke='currentColor' d='M0,1.330 L1.265,0.411 L0.782,-1.067 L-0.782,-1.076 L-1.265,0.411 z'/>\n\t<use xlink:href='#gpPt13' id='gpPt14' fill='currentColor' stroke='none'/>\n\t<filter id='textbox' filterUnits='objectBoundingBox' x='0' y='0' height='1' width='1'>\n\t  <feFlood flood-colo