    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Summary\n\n| Object | Statement |\n|---|---|\n| space | $\\hat{\\mathbb C} = \\mathbb C \\cup \\{\\infty\\} = \\mathbb{CP}^1 = S^2$ |\n| group | $PGL_2(\\mathbb C) = PSL_2(\\mathbb C)$, the Möbius maps |\n| generators | translation, $\\lambda z$, and $1/z$ — or: inversions, in pairs |\n| preserved | circles-and-lines as one family; angles; the cross-ratio |\n| destroyed | distance; the distinction between line and circle |\n| sharp transitivity | on **three** points, so 4 points have one invariant |\n\nThree results were established by computation rather than assertion:\n\n**Circles map to circles**, verified by substituting $z \\mapsto 1/z$ into the general equation and finding $(\\alpha, B, C, \\gamma) \\mapsto (\\gamma, B, -C, \\alpha)$. The $\\alpha \\leftrightarrow \\gamma$ swap is why circles *through the origin* become lines.\n\n**Four points are concyclic iff their cross-ratio is real** — checked on symmetric and asymmetric points of the unit circle, on four collinear points, and on a non-concyclic quadruple. This gives a second, shorter proof of circles-to-circles.\n\n**Stereographic projection turns plane equations into circle equations**, with $\\alpha = c - d$ vanishing exactly when the plane contains the north pole. So a line in the plane is precisely a circle through $\\infty$ — not a convention, a theorem.\n\n## The set-up for hyperbolic geometry\n\nEverything here has been leading somewhere specific. We now have a group acting on a sphere, an invariant (the cross-ratio), and a family of circles with no distinguished member.\n\n**2.06 breaks that symmetry deliberately.** Nominate one circle — the real line $\\mathbb R \\cup \\{\\infty\\}$ — and keep only the Möbius maps that preserve it *and* the upper half-plane above it. Those maps have real coefficients, so the subgroup is\n\n$$PSL_2(\\mathbb R) \\;\\subset\\; PSL_2(\\mathbb C),$$\n\nand it turns out to act on the upper half-plane exactly as an isometry group. Distance is recovered as a logarithm of a cross-ratio taken against the nominated circle.\n\nThis is the Cayley–Klein pattern promised at the end of 2.04, arrived at from the complex side instead of the projective one. The two routes meet, which is the real reason both were worth taking.\n\n## Next\n\n**2.06 — Hyperbolic Geometry** builds the upper half-plane and disc models, derives the metric, and finds that the angles of a triangle sum to less than $\\pi$ — with the deficit *equal to the area*.\n\n## References\n\n- ★ Lee, N.-H. (2020) *Geometry: from Isometries to Special Relativity*. Undergraduate Texts in Mathematics. Cham: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-3-030-42101-4. Develops the Möbius group and the Riemann sphere on the way to hyperbolic geometry and relativity, in the same order used here.\n- ★ Stillwell, J. (2005) *The Four Pillars of Geometry*. Undergraduate Texts in Mathematics. New York: Springer. doi:10.1007/0-387-29052-4. §5.6 on linear fractional functions, and Chapter 8 on the hyperbolic plane, which this notebook prepares.\n- ★ Hartshorne, R. (2000) *Geometry: Euclid and Beyond*. Undergraduate Texts in Mathematics. New York: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-0-387-22676-7. Circular inversion is treated at §37 (the index entry \"inversion, 334\"), as the tool for building the Poincaré models.\n- Needham, T. (1997) *Visual Complex Analysis*. Oxford: Clarendon Press. Chapters 3 and 6 are the best geometric account of Möbius maps, inversion and the Riemann sphere in print.\n- Beardon, A.F. (1983) *The Geometry of Discrete Groups*. Graduate Texts in Mathematics 91. New York: Springer. The systematic treatment of $PSL_2(\\mathbb C)$ acting on $\\hat{\\mathbb C}$, and the standard reference for what follows in 2.06.\n\n---\n*Marked ★ = consulted directly while writing; the rest are standard references given from knowledge, not consulted here. Every code cell in this notebook was executed and its output is the real result; the prose is written from knowledge and checked against these sources where they cover the material.*",
      "metadata": {}
    }
  ]
